      <h1>Sfumato</h1>
      <p class="report-dek">
        A way of painting where edges dissolve into smoke. The word is Italian for "turned to
        smoke" or "vanished like smoke."
      </p>
      <p class="report-byline">Artometrics Glossary · sample style board · scaffold draft</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="history">History</h2>
      <p>
        Sfumato is a Renaissance solution to a perennial problem: hard outlines make a figure
        look cut out, while the world we actually see is full of air, moisture, and scattered
        light. Instead of a clean contour, the painter builds a soft gradient between form and
        ground — thin glazes, subtle value shifts, no sudden line. Leonardo is the name most
        tightly attached to the method, especially in faces and in the atmosphere around them.
        The effect is not blur for its own sake. It is an argument about how vision works.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once you can see it, you start noticing later echoes: romantic haze, pictorialist
        photography, certain kinds of digital portrait retouching, even UI gradients that
        avoid a hard seam. The historical core stays in late-fifteenth- and early-sixteenth-century
        painting, but the style board is useful today because the move — "let the edge breathe" —
        keeps getting rediscovered whenever an image needs to feel observed rather than diagrammed.
      </p>

      <h2 id="figures">Top figures</h2>
      <p>
        Start with Leonardo da Vinci, then look at close contemporaries and later painters who
        absorbed the atmospheric edge: Correggio in the handling of flesh and air, and, in a
        broader sense, later European painting that treats atmosphere as a subject, not a
        backdrop. A finished board should attach each name to a specific work and a short note
        on what that person actually changed — not a generic list of "related artists."
      </p>
      <p>
        See also the encyclopedia entry for
        <a href="/entries/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="gallery">Gallery</h2>
      <p>
        Example plates go here: canonical details, comparison crops (hard contour vs. sfumato),
        and later visual rhymes. Images are placeholders in this scaffold.
      </p>
